\subsection{Diagnostic and Operating Conditions}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} operates on the full matrix residual $B_s = M_{\text{global}}^{-1} M_s - I$; the scalar diagnostic below is its (A2)-simplified form.
Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts} provides a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic: global correction increases bias in subgroup $s$ iff $|\delta_s| / ((1-2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s) > 1$, computable from a pilot sample without estimating treatment effects.
The diagnostic achieves subgroup-level AUC $= 0.86$ under symmetric misclassification and $0.96$ under asymmetric misclassification across 48 FPR/FNR configurations (Figure~\ref{fig:diagnostic_roc}).
In practice, the cost of a false negative (failing to detect bias amplification) exceeds that of a false positive (applying EC-HTE unnecessarily), favoring a recall-oriented threshold.
Under relaxed screening, 79\% of real LLM--dataset pairs show RMSE improvement with coverage $\geq$93\% (Appendix~\ref{app:screening}).

